    "agent_last_message": "The tool is implemented in `output/`. It builds with the harness command, has no dependencies, and passes its tests and an independent cross-check.\n\n**Layout**\n- `output/Cargo.toml`: package manifest with an empty `[dependencies]` table\n- `output/src/main.rs`: argument parsing, streaming file read, JSON output, exit codes\n- `output/src/analysis.rs`: the counting logic\n- `output/tests/cli.rs`: end-to-end tests of exit codes and written files\n- `output/.gitignore`\n\n**Verification**\n- `cargo build --release --manifest-path output/Cargo.toml` builds cleanly from scratch. Clippy with `-D warnings` and rustfmt are clean.\n- All 34 tests pass: 25 unit tests, including feeding input split at every possible chunk boundary, and 9 end-to-end tests.\n- I compared the binary with an independent Python implementation of the spec on 2,266 generated files. They cover mixed case, punctuation, Unicode, every whitespace byte, malformed input, and files larger than the 1 MiB read size. There were no mismatches.\n- The spec's edge cases (empty, whitespace-only, with and without trailing newline, consecutive newlines) produce the expected output.\n- A panic exits with 2. I checked this in a throwaway copy with a forced panic.\n- Performance: a 304 MB file takes about 6\u20137 s here, roughly 45 MB/s single-threaded. Peak memory is about 19 MB regardless of file size. `wc` takes about 1 s on the same file.\n\n**Judgment calls the spec didn't settle**\n1. **\"Malformed\" means invalid UTF-8 or a NUL byte, and exits 1.** Words must go into JSON strings, and invalid bytes can't be represented there without changing counts. NUL catches binary and UTF-16 files, which would otherwise produce garbage words. The consequence is that Latin-1 or Windows-1252 files are rejected rather than counted. If your collection has those, this is the rule to change.\n2. **Case folding is Unicode-aware.** `\u00c9cole` and `\u00e9cole` count as one word. Counting is byte-based, as the spec says.\n3. **Failing to write `--output`** (missing",
